{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\nAn async `verify_completion` run that FAILs returns `findings: null` from\n`gate_status(job_id)`. The caller learns only that the gate failed, never why.\nThe plan-review gate does not behave this way: a failed `review_plan` job returns\na populated `findings` descriptor pointing at its `REVIEW_RESULT` sidecar.\n\nObserved directly this session on ticket `cfe0-4b2b`:\n\n```\n{\"job_id\": \"...\", \"status\": \"failed\", \"gate_type\": \"verify_completion\",\n \"findings\": null, \"verdict\": \"FAIL\"}\n```\n\nThe `COMPLETION_VERDICT` sidecar carrying the real findings HAD been written to the\ntickets branch and was readable by hand. The data was never discarded. It was\nnever surfaced.\n\nCost: the only way to read a completion FAIL's reason is to hand-read the tickets\nbranch, or to re-run a billable gate pass.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThree plan-review-only narrowings on one call-graph seam. The last is the visible\nsymptom; the first two are why a naive fix to the last would not work.\n\n1. `src/rebar/llm/completion_sidecar.py:214` — `emit()` returns a bare `bool`.\n   Its plan-review sibling `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/sidecar.py:94` instead stamps\n   `verdict[\"sidecar_reviewed_at\"] = append_event(...)`, keeping the appended\n   event's timestamp. The completion path discards that timestamp, so no\n   correlation key for the emitted sidecar exists anywhere.\n\n2. `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py:126-127` — `_plan_review_sidecar_fields()` returns `{}`\n   unless `gate_type == \"plan_review\"`. The daemon's terminal index record at\n   `src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py:168` therefore carries neither `sidecar_emitted` nor\n   `sidecar_reviewed_at` for a completion run.\n\n3. `src/rebar/llm/gate_runs.py:201-203` — `_review_result_status()` returns `None`\n   unless `gate_type == _PLAN_REVIEW`. `gate_run_status()` at\n   `src/rebar/llm/gate_runs.py:316-318` only sets `out[\"findings\"]` when that\n   helper returns non-None, so the key is absent for every completion run.\n\nCorrelation by timestamp is what makes the descriptor trustworthy. Reporting the\nnewest `COMPLETION_VERDICT` for the ticket without it would attribute a prior\nrun's findings to this job.\n\n## Design\n\nGeneralise the existing plan-review machinery across both gate types rather than\nadding a parallel completion-only path.\n\n`completion_sidecar.py`: `_append_sidecar_retrying()` returns the timestamp that\n`append_event` returns. `emit()` accepts the verdict dict it already receives and\nstamps `verdict[\"sidecar_reviewed_at\"]` on success, mirroring\n`plan_review/sidecar.py:94`. The return type stays `bool` so no caller changes.\n\n`_mcp_llm.py`: rename `_plan_review_sidecar_fields` to `_gate_sidecar_fields` and\nwiden its guard to admit `verify_completion` alongside `plan_review`. The body\nalready reads only `sidecar_emitted` / `sidecar_reviewed_at`, so no other change.\n\n`gate_runs.py`: add `_COMPLETION_VERDICT = \"COMPLETION_VERDICT\"`. Parameterise\n`_latest_review_result_timestamp` and `_has_review_result_timestamp` by sidecar\nkind plus accepted schema set, keeping their current plan-review behaviour as one\ncase. `_review_result_status` resolves gate type to a\n`(sidecar_kind, schemas)` pair — `plan_review` to `(REVIEW_RESULT,\n{plan_review_result_v1, plan_review_result_v2})`, `verify_completion` to\n`(COMPLETION_VERDICT, {completion_verifier_pass_v1, completion_verifier_fail_v1})`\n— and returns `None` for any other gate type, preserving today's behaviour for\n`code_review`. Its `sidecar_type` output field reports the resolved kind.\n\nExtraction keeps each touched function under its complexity ceiling; the\nresolution table is a module-level mapping, not added branches.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A failed async `verify_completion` job returns a non-null `findings` object from `gate_status`.\n- [ ] That `findings` object reports `sidecar_type` as `COMPLETION_VERDICT`.\n- [ ] That `findings` object reports `readable` as `true` when a matching sidecar exists.\n- [ ] A passed async `verify_completion` job returns a non-null `findings` object.\n- [ ] `completion_sidecar.emit()` stamps `sidecar_reviewed_at` on the verdict dict on success.\n- [ ] `completion_sidecar.emit()` leaves `sidecar_reviewed_at` unset when the append fails.\n- [ ] `_append_sidecar_retrying` returns the timestamp from `append_event`.\n- [ ] The completion index record carries `sidecar_reviewed_at` after a terminal run.\n- [ ] The completion index record carries `sidecar_emitted` after a terminal run.\n- [ ] `_gate_sidecar_fields` returns an empty dict for gate type `code_review`.\n- [ ] `gate_status` on a `code_review` job omits the `findings` key.\n- [ ] A `plan_review` job's `findings` object still reports `sidecar_type` as `REVIEW_RESULT`.\n- [ ] A `plan_review` job's `findings` object still reports `readable` as `true` for a current sidecar.\n- [ ] A plan-review run whose record has no `sidecar_reviewed_at` still reports reason `run-failed`.\n- [ ] A completion sidecar whose timestamp differs from the record's reports `readable` as `false`.\n- [ ] That mismatched case reports a reason naming the missing sidecar.\n- [ ] A completion job in `running` state reports reason `run-running`.\n- [ ] `gate_runs.py` stays under the 800-line module cap.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` reports no new entries.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` reports no increased entries.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` reports no new entries.\n- [ ] `make lint` exits zero.\n- [ ] `make typecheck` exits zero."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789891708305100799,"uuid":"6ab3054c-c1ea-4194-8962-f8737c4ead21"}